## 1.1 The Human Respiration & Photosynthesis Cycle
Twelve occupants (1 adult, 3 children per residential pod across a 3-unit vertical tower) operate as a continuous biological engine, outputting ~1,143 Watts of radiant body heat and an air stream rich in carbon dioxide (~40,000 ppm per exhalation).
Instead of venting this resource into the atmosphere, a low-power, analog-controlled variable speed duct fan siphons this stale, warm air directly out of the residential vertical manifold and injects it into the base of the crop canopy inside the Thermos Greenhouse. The plants scrub the CO₂ (maintaining optimal 800–1,200 ppm levels to boost crop yields by 30% to 50%), while the greenhouse dehumidifier system condenses the airborne moisture into distilled irrigation water.
## 1.2 The Solar Steam Cogeneration (CHP) Loop
To eliminate high-kilowatt electrical water heaters, high-temperature steam from an adjacent 14-foot Parabolic Solar Distiller V1.0 passes directly into a 1" copper riser plumbing array. This steam enters an over-insulated hygiene reservoir inside the Bathing Pod through a submersed copper coil heat exchanger.
The steam dumps its latent heat of vaporization directly into the wash water, heating it to a safe 95°F–100°F without drawing power from the LiFePO4 battery banks.
## 1.3 The Hybrid Thermal Battery Network
An analog bimetallic controller monitors the greenhouse core. If the agriculture pod drops below 42°F during an extended winter storm, it triggers low-voltage 12V cross-chassis circulation pumps.
This siphons 100°F water out of the over-insulated bathing storage tanks and loops it into the greenhouse's under-bed copper plumbing, instantly linking all modules into a massive 1,000+ gallon shared liquid thermal battery, shifting the system's sunless survival window past 100 consecutive hours at -15°F.
