## 2.1 Low-Impact Pillar Matrix

* The Component: 12-inch to 16-inch diameter precast concrete foundation columns are dropped directly into holes prepared via a standard post-hole boring machine or truck-mounted auger.
* Depth Calibration: Pillars are sunk to a baseline depth of 36 to 42 inches (safely penetrating below the local frost line for mid-latitude climates like New York) to entirely eliminate winter frost-heave shifting.
* Adjustable Screw Supports: Heavy-duty, high-capacity adjustable steel screw jackets sit permanently on top of each concrete pillar column. Using a battery-powered commercial impact wrench or a manual leverage rod, field crews can spin the screw threads up or down. This enables a crew to level the entire first-floor chassis plane to within millimeters across uneven, un-graded terrain in under an hour.

## 2.2 Divorced-Stud Multi-Layer Insulation Envelope
To completely halt structural thermal bridging (the transfer of cold/heat through timber studs), the framing relies on a decoupled, double-wall configuration:

* Wall Matrix: Dual independent sub-walls built from 2x4 LSL (Laminated Strand Lumber) are separated by a 4-inch isolated internal air gap, creating an overall wall width of 12 inches.
* Ceiling & Floor Core: Roof and subfloor assemblies utilize heavy 2x12 LSL perimeter frames to accommodate the massive structural loads of vertical stacking.
* Polyurethane Injection: Every wall cavity, subfloor bay, and ceiling channel is filled under pressure with high-density closed-cell polyurethane spray foam (11.0 to 11.25 inches net insulation thickness). This achieves an extraordinary thermal performance profile: R-71.5 for all wall profiles and R-73.1 for floor and ceiling assemblies.

## 2.3 FRP External Composites & Armour Wrap
The entire outer skin of the 10'x14' chassis is encased in high-impact, UV-stabilized Fiber-Reinforced Polymer (FRP) composite panels finished with an automotive-grade gloss gel-coat.

* Thermal Braking: FRP exhibits a thermal conductivity rate roughly 150 to 200 times lower than structural steel, preventing the outer skin from introducing thermal shorts.
* Resilience Metric: The non-porous gel-coat finish shrugs off heavy impacts and casual vandalism. It completely resists corporate solvents, allowing graffiti and spray paint to be chemically stripped off without degrading the underlying fiberglass structural core.
* Electrical Hazard Protection: FRP serves as a native dielectric insulator, protecting occupants from potential lightning strikes or high-voltage DC tracking from the rooftop solar array.

## 2.4 Internal Blind-Mate Cam-Lock Assemblies
Field deployment requires zero traditional nails or framing screws.

* The Lock Mechanism: Heavy steel square bolts are factory-embedded into the wall bases at 16-inch intervals, mating to internal, spring-loaded high-torque cam-lock housings recessed within the floor and ceiling plates.
* Assembly Protocol: Panels are flown or tipped into position. A crew member working entirely from the inside of the room uses a proprietary safety impact socket to crank the internal bolts down. The mechanical cam-lock tightly draws the framing components together, sealing the structural seams in minutes.
* Embedded Conduit Protection: Industrial-grade, multi-pin blind-mate electrical blocks are countersunk right beside the cam-lock assemblies. Surrounding each connection block is a thick, marine-grade rubber O-ring. As the cam-locks are torqued down, these O-rings compress completely. This automatically mates and seals the electrical power circuits inside the wall cavity, keeping all high-voltage wiring paths completely hidden away from scrap metal scavengers.

