## 5.1 Daylighting Ingestion Optics & Core Thermal Storage

* The Glazing Replacement: To achieve an R-30 insulation performance profile, clear glass walls are completely replaced with our standard R-71.5 decoupled LSL composite wall panels. Light is imported through the ceiling using four roof-mounted 14-inch Tubular Daylighting Devices (TDDs).
* Fresnel Ingestion Domes: The exterior acrylic roof collection domes are equipped with internal geometric Fresnel reflectors. These reflectors capture low-angle winter sunlight and direct it down high-reflection aluminum tubes lined with a multi-layer silver film, boasting 99.7% spectral reflectance.
* Uniform PAR Distribution: Prismatic ceiling diffusers scatter full-spectrum Photosynthetically Active Radiation (PAR) evenly across the plant canopy without creating localized hot spots. When this light strikes crop foliage and floor surfaces, it converts directly into trapped infrared heat inside the sealed R-30 envelope.
* Under-Bed Water Matrix: Positioned within the structural dead space beneath the elevated grow benches are six 55-gallon food-grade HDPE drums (300 gallons total volume, weighing ~2,500 lbs). This water mass serves as the module's primary thermal battery, stabilizing interior temperatures.

## 5.2 Dynamic Inter-Pod Respiration and CO₂ Induction Loop

* The Metabolic Inter-Tie: The greenhouse is coupled directly to the adjacent residential stack via an insulated, flexible silicone bellows sleeve that handles structural settling. A low-power 12V variable speed duct fan is integrated into this link.
* Human Respiration Siphoning: The fan draws hot, moist, carbon-dioxide-heavy air (~40,000 ppm CO₂ from human exhalation) out of the residential vertical manifold and introduces it beneath the greenhouse grow benches.
* Photosynthetic Supercharging: This raises and maintains interior carbon dioxide concentrations at an optimized baseline of 800 ppm to 1,200 ppm during daylight hours. This atmospheric enrichment supercharges crop metabolism, increasing overall food and medicine yields by 30% to 50% inside the tight 10'x14' chassis.
* Pure Analog Regulation: The variable-speed fan is governed without microcontrollers or code. A dual-stage bimetallic thermal snap-switch cluster reads exhaust temps. At 70°F (21°C), stage one completes a low-voltage circuit to run the fan at a gentle cruise (consuming 4–6 Watts). If residential temperatures cross 75°F (24°C), stage two engages full voltage to dump excess human body heat directly into the greenhouse buffer.

## 5.3 Passive Dehumidification and Solar Snow-Melter Core

* The Transpiration Trap: As plants photosynthesize, they release massive volumes of water vapor via transpiration. In a highly insulated, sealed structure, relative humidity will rapidly hit 100%, stalling plant growth and causing root rot.
* Automated Mechanical Harvesting: To solve this without energy-hungry commercial units, a 12V high-CFM brushless duct fan draws the humid internal air across a 12"x12" heavy-duty aluminum automotive radiator core. A 12V brushless submersible pump circulates the cold water from the bottom of the under-bed storage drums through this radiator.
* Condensate Recovery: Moisture in the air hits the cold aluminum core, condenses instantly, and drops into an integrated under-bed drip pan. This distilled water is piped directly into the irrigation reservoirs, automatically regulating daytime humidity.
* The Zero-Watt Snow Melter: The warm, dry air exiting the dehumidifier core is continuously pushed through a back-pressure manifold that leads straight into the insulated aluminum baffles behind the 40° solar panel array. This dry, 70°F air warms the underside of the solar glass, causing heavy winter snow blankets to break their ice bond and slide off automatically, completely preserving the off-grid generation cycle.

